{
"dur": 61.014,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 11.4,
"text": "After Alexander the Great, Greek kings ruled Egypt. Around 270 BC, Ptolemy the Second reopened the canal, and his engineers added a gate that stopped salty sea water flowing into the Nile's fresh water.",
"end": 11.675
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 11.675,
"vo_start": 11.95,
"vo_end": 18.43,
"text": "Some historians call it the first canal lock in history. It protected the farms of the Nile, which fed the whole country.",
"end": 18.705
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 18.705,
"vo_start": 18.98,
"vo_end": 27.54,
"text": "Rome took Egypt and kept the canal going. Under Emperor Trajan, barges full of Egyptian grain sailed along it. Rome ate Egyptian bread.",
"end": 27.815
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 27.815,
"vo_start": 28.09,
"vo_end": 39.649,
"text": "When Rome weakened, nobody paid to clear the sand. The Arab general Amr ibn al-As reopened the canal around 640, but about a hundred and thirty years later a caliph had it blocked on purpose, to cut off rebels in Arabia.",
"end": 39.924
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 39.924,
"vo_start": 40.199,
"vo_end": 45.154,
"text": "For the next thousand years, the canal was gone. Even its route was forgotten.",
"end": 45.429
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 45.429,
"vo_start": 45.704,
"vo_end": 60.064,
"text": "In the 1500s, Portugal's ships went round Africa and took the spice trade. In Istanbul, the Ottoman grand vizier Sokollu Mehmed Pasha planned a canal to fight back. The plan was shelved. The empire had too many wars.",
"end": 61.014
}
],
